<PageTitle>Sources &amp; Methods | Nuclear Evaluation</PageTitle>

<div class="sources-page">
    <header class="sources-header">
        <h1>Sources &amp; Methods</h1>
        <p>Where the radiation observations, radionuclide measurements, fleet figures, and emissions estimates come from.</p>
    </header>

    <section id="radnet" class="sources-card sources-archive" aria-labelledby="sources-radnet-title">
        <header class="sources-card-header">
            <span class="sources-icon" aria-hidden="true"><RadzenIcon Icon="sensors" /></span>
            <h2 id="sources-radnet-title">U.S. EPA RadNet archive</h2>
            <p>The Radiation Monitoring workspace uses historical observations from EPA’s fixed air monitoring stations. Explore by station, annual source file, observation, and reported gamma channel.</p>
        </header>

        <dl class="sources-definitions sources-archive-definitions">
            <div>
                <dt>Gamma count rate <span>CPM</span></dt>
                <dd>Counts per minute measured from particles collected on a station’s air filter. The archive reports separate gamma channel ranges.</dd>
            </div>
            <div>
                <dt>Gamma exposure rate <span>mR/h</span></dt>
                <dd>Exposure to gamma radiation in air around the station, reported in milliroentgens per hour.</dd>
            </div>
            <div>
                <dt>Dose equivalent rate <span>nSv/h</span></dt>
                <dd>EPA’s dose equivalent rate in nanosieverts per hour, available in some station files.</dd>
            </div>
        </dl>

        <p class="sources-method">
            Observation timestamps use UTC. Missing measurements stay blank. File quality counts hours with no reported observation within the file’s covered period and shows how often each channel was reported.
            The workspace keeps count rates, exposure rates, and dose equivalent rates in their published units.
        </p>
        <p class="sources-method">
            Import checks exclude rows with invalid timestamps, timestamps outside the source year, or no usable measurement.
            For repeated station timestamps, the import prefers an approved row, then the row with the most measurements, then the first source row.
            Retained records carry their source file and original row number.
        </p>

        <ul class="sources-references" aria-label="EPA RadNet sources" role="list">
            <li><a href="https://www.epa.gov/radnet/radnet-csv-file-downloads">Historical station archives <span>Annual CSVs</span></a></li>
            <li><a href="https://www.epa.gov/radnet/how-use-radnet-near-real-time-air-data-dashboard">Monitoring definitions <span>U.S. EPA</span></a></li>
            <li><a href="https://edg.epa.gov/EPA_Data_License.html">EPA data license <span>U.S. public domain</span></a></li>
            <li><a href="https://www.epa.gov/office-inspector-general/follow-report-epa-improves-management-its-radiation-monitoring-system">Montgomery monitor cover photograph <span>EPA OIG, 2014</span></a></li>
        </ul>

        <div class="sources-actions">
            <a class="sources-action" href="radiation">Explore radiation monitoring <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a>
        </div>
    </section>

    <section id="remdb" class="sources-card sources-archive" aria-labelledby="sources-remdb-title">
        <header class="sources-card-header">
            <span class="sources-icon" aria-hidden="true"><RadzenIcon Icon="science" /></span>
            <h2 id="sources-remdb-title">European Commission REMdb archive</h2>
            <p>Radiochemistry uses measurements reported by national monitoring programs and published by the European Commission’s Joint Research Centre in its Radioactivity Environmental Monitoring data bank.</p>
        </header>

        <dl class="sources-definitions sources-archive-definitions">
            <div>
                <dt>Sampling locations</dt>
                <dd>Locations are grouped by the reported country, place, and coordinates.</dd>
            </div>
            <div>
                <dt>Sampling windows</dt>
                <dd>Measurements are grouped by location, medium, treatment, and sampling timestamps. A window may contain results from several physical samples.</dd>
            </div>
            <div>
                <dt>Measurements</dt>
                <dd>Each result keeps its reported activity and unit, along with its source file and row number.</dd>
            </div>
        </dl>

        <p class="sources-method">
            This snapshot covers 40 annual releases from 1984 through 2023, drawn from the 1984–2006 and 2007–2011 bundles and the 2012–2023 annual archives.
            Sampling dates can differ from release years. Files from 2012 onward publish a separate detection qualifier field.
            File quality reports show the sampling dates covered, missing values, and explicit less-than qualifiers in each file.
        </p>
        <p class="sources-method">
            The 1986 file splits some measurements across consecutive CSV rows. The import rejoins recognized pairs and cites the first row.
        </p>
        <p class="sources-method">
            Data © European Union. Nuclear Evaluation adapts the public files by parsing them into searchable fields and grouping the reported records. The source data are published under Creative Commons Attribution 4.0.
        </p>

        <ul class="sources-references" aria-label="European Commission REMdb sources" role="list">
            <li><a href="https://jeodpp.jrc.ec.europa.eu/ftp/jrc-opendata/REMDB/datasets/">REMdb archive <span>Public releases</span></a></li>
            <li><a href="https://jeodpp.jrc.ec.europa.eu/ftp/jrc-opendata/REMDB/datasets/copyright.txt">REMdb reuse terms <span>CC BY 4.0</span></a></li>
            <li><a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/">Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 <span>License</span></a></li>
            <li><a href="https://essd.copernicus.org/articles/11/589/2019/">REMdb methods and data description <span>Joint Research Centre</span></a></li>
        </ul>

        <div class="sources-actions">
            <a class="sources-action" href="radiochemistry">Explore radiochemistry <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a>
        </div>
    </section>

    <div class="sources-published">
        <section class="sources-card" aria-labelledby="sources-fleet-title">
            <header class="sources-card-header">
                <span class="sources-icon" aria-hidden="true"><RadzenIcon Icon="map" /></span>
                <h2 id="sources-fleet-title">U.S. nuclear fleet</h2>
                <p>The 2024 snapshot covers nuclear generators that the U.S. Energy Information Administration lists as operating at year-end.</p>
            </header>

            <dl class="sources-definitions">
                <div>
                    <dt>Net summer capacity <span>MW</span></dt>
                    <dd>Maximum power a generator can supply during summer peak demand, after accounting for power used by the plant.</dd>
                </div>
                <div>
                    <dt>Net generation <span>MWh or GWh</span></dt>
                    <dd>Electricity generated over time, after subtracting the electricity used by the plant. A gigawatt-hour is 1,000 megawatt-hours.</dd>
                </div>
            </dl>

            <p class="sources-method">
                Plant-month totals add the reported values for included reactors. A month with no reported values stays blank.
                Negative values are preserved, and an annual plant total requires 12 reported months.
                The Generation Calendar divides the reported state total by the number of days in the month for its daily average.
            </p>

            <ul class="sources-references" aria-label="Fleet datasets" role="list">
                <li>
                    <a href="https://www.eia.gov/electricity/data/eia860/archive/xls/eia8602024.zip">
                        EIA-860 generator inventory <span>2024 ZIP</span>
                    </a>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <a href="https://www.eia.gov/nuclear/generation/archive/xls/usreact24.xlsx">
                        EIA reactor generation workbook <span>2024 XLSX</span>
                    </a>
                </li>
            </ul>

            <p class="sources-map-note">
                State outlines use U.S. Census cartographic boundaries distributed by
                <a href="https://github.com/topojson/us-atlas" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">U.S. Atlas</a>.
                <a href="data/fleet-map-license.txt">Map license</a>
            </p>
            <div class="sources-actions">
                <a class="sources-action" href="fleet">Explore the fleet <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a>
                <a class="sources-action" href="fleet-generation">Open the generation calendar <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a>
